# Migration files are checked out with LF on every platform, deliberately.
#
# Prisma records a SHA-256 of each migration.sql in _prisma_migrations and
# compares it on every run. That hash is taken over the file's BYTES, so line
# endings are part of it. With core.autocrlf=true and no rule here, Windows
# checkouts get CRLF while Linux gets LF, and the two platforms therefore
# compute DIFFERENT checksums for a file neither of them edited.
#
# What that looked like in practice: four migrations were recorded with their LF
# hash, Git later rewrote the working copy to CRLF, and Prisma began reporting
# them as modified after they were applied. No SQL had changed - all thirteen
# files matched either the LF or the CRLF form of their stored hash exactly.
# `prisma migrate status` does NOT surface this; it reported "Database schema is
# up to date!" throughout. The damage is that `migrate dev` responds to the
# mismatch by offering to reset the database, and `migrate deploy` refuses to
# run at all.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes the bytes identical on Edward's Windows checkout and in
# Koshi's Linux container, so a migration applied on one machine verifies on the
# other.
*.sql text eol=lf

# The SAME bug, in a second place, found 2026-09-24.
#
# EAS computes a `fingerprint` runtime version by hashing the bytes of the files
# that decide what the native app contains - eas.json, app.json, .gitignore and
# the native side of node_modules. An over-the-air update is only served to a
# build whose fingerprint matches, so the two have to agree exactly.
#
# The APK is built on EAS's Linux machines, where eas.json is LF. `eas update`
# computes the fingerprint from the LOCAL checkout, which on Windows was CRLF.
# Twenty-four carriage returns in a file nobody had edited were enough to turn
# a1d292d6 into df761dab, and an update published from this laptop would have
# uploaded happily and then been served to no one - the phone asks for its own
# runtime version and never sees a reply.
#
# `eas fingerprint:compare` reports this as `modified file: eas.json`, which
# reads like someone changed it. `git ls-files --eol` is what actually shows it:
# `i/lf  w/crlf`. Note that `grep -q $'\r'` answered NO on a file that od proves
# is full of them - check the bytes, not a grep.
mobile/eas.json text eol=lf
mobile/app.json text eol=lf
mobile/.gitignore text eol=lf

# Added with push notifications (2026-09-25). app.json's `googleServicesFile`
# points at this, and it is copied into the native project at prebuild, so it
# helps decide what the built app contains - the same class of input as the
# three above. It is LF today; this keeps it LF on a fresh Windows clone rather
# than waiting to rediscover the bug above from the other end.
mobile/google-services.json text eol=lf
